<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joya de Cerén</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200–600</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Pompeii of the Americas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joya de Cerén is an archaeological site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-libertad-region">La Libertad Department</a>, El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>, holding the remains of a pre-Columbian Maya farming village about 36 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>. Thought to have been occupied between 200 and 600 CE, the village of an estimated 200 people was buried by ash from an eruption of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loma-people">Loma</a> Caldera around 600, apparently after its people had fled, for no human remains have been found. The ash preserved its houses, ceremonial buildings, stored food and fields, among them a manioc field, the earliest known evidence of manioc cultivation in the New World. Found by a bulldozer driver in 1976, it was inscribed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List in 1993.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joya-de-ceren.jpg" alt="Joya de Cerén" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mariordo (Mario Roberto Duran Ortiz) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ES_Joya_Ceren_05_2012_Estructura_9_Area_2_Tamazcal_wide_angle_1479.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archaeology of houses and burials, as at Copán and the buried village of <b>Joya de Cerén</b>, shows how ordinary people lived.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classic-maya">Social Hierarchy of the Classic Maya</a></li></ul></section>
